# A member card links to /members/<username>, and this is what serves the
# profile page for it. The username travels in the query string because the
# path does not survive: Cloudflare answers this rule with a 308 to the
# destination rather than rewriting in place -- measured on the live site,
# /members/ayse came back 308 -> /members/profile/ -- so the browser's URL
# becomes the destination and anything only in the original path is lost.
# profile.astro then read "profile" as the username and bounced back to
# /members, which looked to the reader like the page simply reloading.
#
# Carrying it as :username makes the rule right under either behaviour: as a
# rewrite the address bar keeps /members/ayse, as a redirect it becomes
# /members/profile/?u=ayse. Both give the page the name it needs.
#
# The trailing slash matters too. Astro builds directory-style pages, so
# /members/profile is itself a 308 to /members/profile/ -- pointing at the
# unslashed form meant two redirects where one would do.
/members/:username  /members/profile/?u=:username  200

